    "slug": "nusoj-report-only-10-of-somali-women-journalists-feel-safe-at-work",
    "summary": "A new report by the National Union of Somali Journalists reveals that just one in ten women journalists in Somalia feels safe at work, highlighting widespread harassment and discrimination.",
    "content": "The National Union of Somali Journalists (NUSOJ) released a report on Monday revealing that only one in ten women journalists in Somalia feels safe in their workplace. The report, titled \"Unsafe, Underpaid and Unheard: The Workplace Crisis Facing Women Journalists in Somalia,\" was launched to coincide with the International Day to End Impunity for Crimes against Journalists. It draws on a survey of women journalists in Mogadishu and Somalia's six regions, though the full methodology was not detailed in the source.\n\nThe findings point to a pervasive culture of harassment, exploitation, and discrimination within the media industry. According to NUSOJ, the survey results highlight an urgent need for action to address these conditions. The report's title underscores the multifaceted challenges women journalists face: not only physical safety concerns but also economic precarity and a lack of institutional support.\n\nNUSOJ has previously documented threats against journalists in Somalia, but this report focuses specifically on gender-based issues. The union has called for immediate measures to end the \"appalling culture\" identified in the survey. The report's release on the International Day to End Impunity for Crimes against Journalists underscores the global context of violence and impunity that journalists face, with women often disproportionately affected.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the report's findings resonate deeply. Many diaspora members maintain close ties to media outlets in Somalia or work in journalism themselves. The unsafe conditions described may discourage diaspora women from pursuing careers in Somali media or returning to contribute their skills. Additionally, a media environment where women's voices are silenced or marginalized can limit the diversity of perspectives available to diaspora audiences seeking reliable news about their homeland.\n\nNUSOJ's report adds to growing calls for reforms within Somalia's media sector. While the union has not announced specific follow-up actions, the survey's results are likely to fuel advocacy for stronger protections and accountability mechanisms. The international community, including diaspora organizations, may play a role in supporting such efforts.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/11/02/only-one-in-ten-women-journalists-feel-safe-at-work-in-somalia-nusoj-report-finds/?utm_source=rss&#038;utm_medium=rss&#038;utm_campaign=only-one-in-ten-women-journalists-feel-safe-at-work-in-somalia-nusoj-report-finds",
