    "content": "Mogadishu, November 2, 2026 — The Somali Women in Media Association (SOMWA) presented a report in Mogadishu on Monday documenting the detention, harassment, and online attacks faced by media workers in Somalia, with a particular focus on women. The report was released to coincide with the International Day to End Impunity for Crimes against Journalists.\n\nThe report highlights that women journalists are disproportionately affected by digital violence, including online harassment and threats, in addition to physical detention and other forms of pressure. SOMWA Director Maryan Seylac called for credible investigations into abuses against journalists and for those responsible to be held accountable.\n\nSomalia has a history of attacks on journalists, but women in the profession often face additional gender-based threats. SOMWA's report aims to draw attention to these specific challenges, which have been less documented in the past.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, which includes many journalists and media consumers, the report underscores the risks faced by colleagues in Somalia and the importance of supporting efforts to protect press freedom. Diaspora media outlets often rely on local journalists for information, and attacks on them can have a chilling effect on reporting.\n\nSOMWA's call for accountability comes amid ongoing concerns about the safety of media workers in Somalia. The group's report is part of broader efforts to document and address violations against journalists, particularly women, and to promote a safer environment for the press.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/11/02/haweenka-suxufiyiinta-soomaaliya-oo-weerarro-wajahaya/?utm_source=rss&#038;utm_medium=rss&#038;utm_campaign=haweenka-suxufiyiinta-soomaaliya-oo-weerarro-wajahaya",
