    "summary": "Uganda's military has started pulling out troops deployed in Somalia under a bilateral agreement with the Somali government, with the first contingent already returned home.",
    "content": "Uganda's military has initiated the withdrawal of its troops stationed in Somalia under a bilateral agreement with the Somali federal government, according to a statement released on Monday by Uganda People's Defence Forces (UPDF) spokesman Chris Magezi. The pullout, which is effective immediately, is being conducted in phases. The first group of soldiers has already returned to Uganda, and further withdrawals will be coordinated with Somali authorities.\n\nThe UPDF has been a key contributor to the African Union Mission in Somalia (AMISOM), now the African Union Transition Mission in Somalia (ATMIS), which has been supporting Somali forces against al-Shabaab militants. However, the troops being withdrawn now are those deployed separately under a bilateral arrangement, not as part of the AU mission. This distinction is important as it indicates a shift in Uganda's direct military engagement in Somalia.\n\nThe decision to withdraw follows months of discussions between Kampala and Mogadishu over the status of these forces. While the exact reasons for the withdrawal have not been publicly detailed, it comes amid ongoing efforts to transition security responsibilities to Somali forces. Uganda has been a major troop contributor to the AU mission, and its bilateral deployment has been seen as complementary to that effort.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this withdrawal could have significant implications. Many in the diaspora have family members in Somalia and rely on stability for investments and travel. A reduction in foreign troops may raise concerns about security gaps, particularly in areas where Ugandan forces have been active. However, it also signals growing confidence in Somali-led security, a point often emphasized by the federal government.\n\nAnalysts suggest that the withdrawal may be part of a broader realignment of Uganda's military priorities, but it remains unclear how it will affect the overall security landscape. The Somali government has not yet commented on the withdrawal, but officials have previously stated that bilateral arrangements are subject to review. The phased nature of the pullout suggests a measured approach to avoid creating a vacuum.\n\nAs the drawdown proceeds, the diaspora will be watching closely to see how it impacts security and stability in the regions where Ugandan troops were deployed. The international community has called for a smooth transition to ensure that gains made against al-Shabaab are not reversed.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/uganda-withdrawing-troops-deployed-bilaterally-somalia/",
    "source": "somaliguardian.com",
