    "summary": "Uganda has officially started reducing and withdrawing its troops serving in the African Union mission in Somalia, with plans to complete the exit by 2027.",
    "content": "Uganda's government has announced that it has begun the process of reducing and withdrawing its military personnel deployed in Somalia as part of the African Union mission. The phased drawdown is scheduled to conclude by 2027, according to official statements. Uganda is a major troop-contributing country to the AU mission, which supports Somali security forces in combating al-Shabaab.\n\nThe withdrawal follows the United Nations Security Council's decision to gradually transfer security responsibilities to Somali forces. The African Union Transition Mission in Somalia (ATMIS) is mandated to exit by the end of 2024, but the Ugandan plan extends beyond that timeline, suggesting a longer-term approach. Uganda's contingent has been active in Somalia since 2007, playing a key role in operations against al-Shabaab.\n\nUgandan officials have not specified the exact number of troops to be withdrawn or the timeline for each phase. However, the announcement aligns with broader discussions within the AU and international partners on the post-ATMIS security architecture. The Somali government has expressed commitment to taking over security responsibilities, but concerns remain about the readiness of Somali forces.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the withdrawal raises questions about security back home. Many diaspora members have investments and family ties in Somalia and closely monitor stability. A potential security vacuum could affect remittances and diaspora engagement. Some community leaders urge continued international support to prevent gains from being reversed.\n\nThe phased withdrawal is likely to be closely watched by regional and international stakeholders. Uganda's decision may influence other troop-contributing countries. The Somali diaspora is encouraged to stay informed through reliable sources and engage in dialogue on security and development issues. The coming months will reveal the pace and impact of the drawdown.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/uganda-oo-bilowday-ka-bixitaanka-ciidamadeeda-soomaaliya-qorshuhuna-yahay-in-la-soo-afjaro-joogitaankooda-2027/427105/",
